// Test: `#[…]` — an array whose mix of element types is DECLARED.
//
// Decision 15 of Divergente_ES/forma/README.md: `[…]` is homogeneous and gets
// checked; `#[…]` declares the mix and does not. **They are the same type** and
// every operator behaves the same. The `#` is the meta/type mark, so declaring
// that an array has an open element type is a statement about its type.
//
// `#?` tells them apart from v0.0.9 — `##]` when the elements are all one type
// and `##[` when they are not — and that is NOT a second type. It is read from
// what the array HOLDS when asked, never from how the literal was written, and
// the difference shows up twice below: an array built by `json::decode` answers
// `##[` with no mark anywhere in the program, and taking the mix out of a
// `#[…]` leaves an array that answers `##]`, because one Int is not a mix.
//
// This is what closes the hole the rule used to leave: `json::decode` hands back
// a heterogeneous array, and until now there was no way to WRITE one — the
// language could produce a value it could not spell (decision 17).
//
// It also closed DM-04. `[1, "dos", 3.0]` was refused by both Rust engines and
// accepted by the browser one, which checked nothing at all; the refusal is
// pinned in reject/collections/04_heterogeneous_literal.zy. And `#[` was a
// syntax error in the two Rust engines while the browser lexer **dropped the
// `#` without a sound**, so `#[…]` there was just a plain `[…]`.

// A declared mix of four different types.
m = #[#0, 1, '2', "tres", 4.0]
>> m ¶
>> m$# ¶

// `#?` reports the mix; the TYPE is still the array type, which is what every
// line after this one demonstrates.
>> m#? ¶
h = [1, 2, 3]
>> h#? ¶

// Read from the contents and not from the literal: take the mix out and it is
// an array again, mark or no mark.
sin_mezcla = #[1, "dos"]$-[2]
>> sin_mezcla " " (sin_mezcla#?)[1] ¶

// And they are still equal, still the same type, still one collection.
>> ([1, 2] == #[1, 2]) ¶
uniforme_marcado = #[1, 2]
>> (uniforme_marcado#?)[1] ¶

// Every operator behaves the same on both.
>> (m$? 1) ¶
>> m[2] ¶
>> m[-1] ¶
>> m$[2..3] ¶

// It is an ordinary array: the edit family works, and in place (decision 12).
m$+ "cinco"
>> m ¶
m[1]$~ #1
>> m ¶

// Nested, and mixed at both levels.
n = #[[1, 2], "medio", #[#1, 3.5]]
>> n ¶
>> n[3][2] ¶

// An empty `#[]` is the empty array — nothing to declare, nothing to check.
e = #[]
>> e " " e$# ¶

// The homogeneous case is deliberately absent: `#[1, 2, 3]` warns (decision 18,
// the vaccine against what `Object` did to Java and `any` to TypeScript), and
// the browser harness does not print warnings at all — that is DM-07, not this
// file's subject. A case that failed for that reason would not be measuring
// `#[…]`.
